# Environments and caches
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# outputs/ is PARTIALLY tracked. This is deliberate.
#
# Tracked, because the pipeline cannot regenerate them or because the report is
# bound to them: every *_metrics.json, the frozen windows.json and
# target_intervals.json, molecules.json, rollout_bank.json, provenance.json,
# configs_used.json, and the trained head checkpoints (*.pt).
#
# tests/test_report_matches_artifacts.py re-reads those JSON files and asserts the
# numbers appear in reports/pilot_report.md, so dropping them would silently disable
# 16 tests.
#
# The frozen windows.json and target_intervals.json matter most: they were fixed
# BEFORE any guided result was inspected, and regenerating them on different hardware
# could shift them. Carrying them lets the next machine detect that rather than
# quietly re-derive new ones.
#
# Excluded below: the large arrays from scripts/02_generate_trajectories.py, which are
# reproduced deterministically from the pinned model revision and recorded seeds.
# Re-running step 2 of docs/REPRODUCE.md rebuilds them. Committing ~700 MB to avoid an
# hour of GPU time is not a good trade. See docs/KICKOFF_PROMPT.md §1.
#
# REVISED 2026-08-03, after C17/C23-C29 grew outputs/ to 25 GB. The rule above was
# written when the only bulk arrays were named hidden.npy / features.npy. The layer and
# window state caches added since (`layer*.npy`, `states*.npy`) are the same kind of
# object under different names and were not covered, so the two lines were generalised
# to every .npy / .npz under outputs/. That is 23.7 GB of the 25 GB.
#
# The one caveat that must travel with this decision: §11.1 and claim CL11 record that
# CUDA draws a *different* 50k sample from CPU at the same seed, so these caches are
# reproducible only on hardware whose RNG stream matches. Dropping them is therefore a
# real loss of exact reproducibility, accepted because (a) every published number is
# read from a *_metrics.json that IS tracked, (b) the frozen windows.json and
# target_intervals.json are tracked so a re-derivation on new hardware can be *detected*
# rather than silently accepted, and (c) 23.7 GB is not a thing to put in git.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
outputs/**/*.npy
outputs/**/*.npz
outputs/**/*.csv
outputs/**/trajectories.json
outputs/**/prefix_token_ids.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Head checkpoints (*.pt), 538 files / 795 MB as of 2026-08-03.
#
# The default is now EXCLUDE, with an allowlist below, reversing the blanket "and the
# trained head checkpoints (*.pt)" above. The rule applied: a checkpoint is tracked if a
# test loads it, or if it is the head a *deployed* published configuration was run with.
# A checkpoint from a sweep whose every published number already lives in a tracked
# *_metrics.json is not tracked -- the sweep's conclusion survives without it.
#
# What this drops, and why each is judged safe:
#   c25_pooled_heads/       489 MB, 198 files. C25 is a prediction-only sweep; every
#                           AUROC it reports is in c25_summary/. No steering result
#                           depends on these, and its headline (the 25x) was retracted
#                           by C29 anyway.
#   c29_heads/              55 MB. Eight head seeds x seven arms; C29's numbers are all
#                           in c29_summary/c29_metrics.json, which is tracked.
#   c24_probe_layers/heads/ 39 GPT-2 text heads. C24 is the external-validity side
#                           experiment; c24_summary/ carries it.
#   pilot_50k_heads_p2_legacymask/  56 MB. These are the *defective* pre-fix heads from
#                           the §11.5 interval-mask bug. §11.6 already quantified what
#                           they cost; carrying the broken checkpoints adds nothing.
#   c18_heads_*/, c29_c27heads_*/   C18's readout variants and C29's C27 replications.
#
# What is kept: phase-1 heads (already tracked), the deployed phase-2 heads, and the
# C17 probe-point heads -- the last because tests/test_pooled_readout.py and
# tests/test_layer_end_to_end.py load head_aromatic_rings_frozen_state_L12.pt directly,
# and because C23's reproduction commands in docs/REPRODUCE.md name the L3/L4/L6 files.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
outputs/**/*.pt
!outputs/pilot_10k_heads/*.pt
!outputs/pilot_50k_heads/*.pt
!outputs/pilot_50k_heads_p2/*.pt
!outputs/pilot_50k_dagger_clogp/*.pt
!outputs/c17_probe_layers/*.pt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-run molecule dumps and run logs, added 2026-08-03 when C30-C33 were committed.
#
# The header above lists `molecules.json` as tracked. That was written when the project
# had four of them. C23-C33 produced 309, totalling 303 MB, and committing them would
# have taken .git from 21 MB to over 500 MB in a single commit -- a decision that cannot
# be undone without rewriting history.
#
# The rule applied is the same one the *.pt allowlist uses: an artefact is tracked if a
# test reads it, or if it belongs to a *deployed* published configuration. No test in
# tests/ reads a molecules.json; every published hit rate, validity and token count is
# read from a tracked *_metrics.json. The four already-tracked dumps (phase 1 and the
# pilot guided runs) stay tracked -- a .gitignore rule does not untrack a tracked file --
# so the worked examples a reader needs are still in the repository.
#
# What this costs: re-running scripts/09, 10, 18, 23, 24, 26 or the C24/C31 gates on a
# fresh clone requires regenerating the dumps first, exactly as the .npy caches above do.
# What it does not cost: any number in reports/, any figure, or any of the 799 tests.
#
# Logs are excluded for the same reason and a weaker one -- they are console transcripts,
# 6 MB of them, and nothing reads them. The 8 already-tracked logs stay tracked.
#
# c24_dataset/sequences.json (14.7 MB) is the raw GPT-2 text corpus for C24, regenerable
# from scripts/19_c24_dataset.py at the recorded seed; c24_summary/ carries every number.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
outputs/**/molecules.json
outputs/**/*.log
outputs/c24_dataset/sequences.json
